<p>Iran’s political system is entering a high-stakes transition after Supreme Leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei was killed in U.S.-Israeli strikes, according to reports. Iranian state media said cleric Alireza Arafi was appointed to an interim Leadership Council to carry out the supreme leader’s duties until a permanent successor is chosen. Several outlets describe the succession as constitutionally guided but politically fraught. The Assembly of Experts is formally responsible for selecting the next supreme leader, even as Iran faces continued strikes and mounting internal strain. Reporting that cites senior U.S. officials also suggests deep skepticism inside the U.S. government that any U.S.-backed opposition figure could quickly take control in Tehran, even as some U.S. messaging has floated the idea of regime change.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Pl6wCLnnQuJ65WlDfbk6kb4mUXNxgzybpcV9Mfv4tb53I2ItNtuyc10cO0jbyU7JBTy5WFBEMHC9389CCd6ouE_I0OQPIn-XoDJwxjeJbtmBVx0JtoY21LU3BRvcV4ahGMwmUo-PqXFmISqw2pxwMMdUlY_XLuyaYSbtRebzeUGvrSR7vIyXeKg8indE5g' alt='A giant billboard featuring a portrait of Ayatollah Ali Khamenei in Tehran’s Valiasr Square (file photo).' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Scale of losses: Reports say the strikes that killed Khamenei also killed about 40 other senior members of the Islamic Republic regime, including top commanders.</li><li>Interim arrangement: Iranian state media said Alireza Arafi—described by ISNA as a member of the Guardian Council—was named to the interim body handling the supreme leader’s responsibilities until a successor is appointed.</li><li>Formal selector: Under Iran’s constitution, the Assembly of Experts—an 88-member clerical body—chooses the next supreme leader and can also remove one, a power it has never used.</li><li>Backdrop of crisis: Reporting describes the succession fight unfolding during what it calls Iran’s most perilous crisis since the 1979 revolution, with war on its own territory and rising internal strain.</li><li>Behind-the-scenes politics: Several accounts stress that the formal constitutional process is only part of the story; bargaining among clerical and security power centers is expected to heavily shape who ultimately emerges as supreme leader.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Iranian state media: Iran named Ayatollah Alireza Arafi to an interim Leadership Council, alongside President Masoud Pezeshkian and Chief Justice Gholamhossein Mohseni Ejei, to carry out the supreme leader’s duties until the Assembly of Experts selects a successor. (<a href='https://english.alarabiya.net/News/middle-east/2026/03/01/iran-names-alireza-arafi-to-leadership-council-after-khamenei-killing'>Al Arabiya English</a>)</li><li>President Donald Trump: Reuters-cited reporting says Trump and some U.S. officials have publicly floated toppling Iran’s governing system as a U.S. goal. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/us-officials-skeptical-regime-change-tehran-after-khamenei-killing-say-sources'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Senior U.S. officials (sources): Reuters-cited sources say many senior U.S. officials remain skeptical that the military operation will lead to near-term regime change. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/us-officials-skeptical-regime-change-tehran-after-khamenei-killing-say-sources'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>RT analysis: RT argues the conflict does not end with Khamenei’s death and could shift into a longer, principle-driven confrontation that raises the odds of a broader war. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/news/633367-jihad-is-coming-khameneis-death/'>RT</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/inside-irans-succession-process-and-who-could-be-the-countrys-next-supreme-leader'>Inside Iran&#x27;s succession process and who could be the country&#x27;s next supreme leader</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/6fe57836-7392-471f-875d-293c7f2db7d4'>After Khamenei, who rules 